WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  so  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii irene s  was born in 1925 in berezani of zajani  galicia now ukraine and was raised in  grujon's poland  she was a survivor of the bialystok  ghetto and several concentration camps  including majdanek treblinka and  auschwitz irene was a slave laborer  in germany and was liberated by the  american in counties  by the time the war broke out irene  already was an accomplished singer and  songwriter  in her testimony she recalls singing  existing songs  and composing new ones together with her  young fellow prisoners  from poland and the soviet union the  poem sachisaki otherwise known as anima  amin  i believe was written by the poet  translator physician and writer chaul  chavnichowski  in odessa ukraine then russian empire in  1892  czernikovsky was born in ukraine to a  modern religious family  studied languages in odessa where he  became involved in zionist activities  czernikovsky served as a doctor in  russian army during world war one  and then pursuing his dream he  repatriated  or immigrated to the land of israel to  defend palestine in 1931.  i believe is one of chernikovsky's very  early poems  permeated by idealistic feelings the  young poet  unites the ideals of liberty fraternity  peace and zionist ideals the jewish  people's hope and aspiration to  freely live in their ancestral land the  land of israel  it is really moving and symbolic how all  these ideals strongly resonated with the  feelings  of the young inmates at the ghettos and  camps  among which was irene the songs like  this help  them survive and make it to the  liberation day  [Music]  a  [Music]  [Music]  mommy  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  oh  [Music]  [Applause]  foreign  foreign  [Music]  in her testimony irene recalls that  while being transported from the  bialystol ghetto  to an unknown destination she  and other young people in the catholic  began singing  they sang as she says patriotic songs  sion his songs we were singing ourselves  to go to die she says one of these songs  whose melody she wrote herself was in  russian  because she says the boy who wrote the  words  didn't know any yiddish they did not  sing  for long because the guards on the train  shouted to them to stop and fired into  the cattle car  wounding irene and killing one of the  lads  the transport passed by the  extermination camp treblinka  and she remembers seeing vast fields  covered with ashes and bones  but they went on to lublin and she  subsequently  was taken to the nearby concentration  and extermination camp  maidanik yet irene also remembers  that the young men and women in the  cattle car sang another song  and she beautifully sings a few verses  of it  the song is known as saki saki  although its original name is i believe  anima amin  irene does not mention the name of the  song  and erroneously attributes it to haim  nachman bialik  in fact it is based on a poem written by  shaolin  another important jewish zionist poet  from ukraine  who like bialik ended up living in  palestine  czernikovsky was born in a small  ukrainian village  in 1875 and wrote sakisaki  in 1894 when he was merely 19 years old  at the time he was living in odessa and  already knew  both russian and hebrew he subsequently  also learned german english and french  as well  as ancient greek and latin a man of  great talent and energy  he studied medicine at the universities  of heidelberg  lausanne and kiev while studying at  heidelberg  czernikovsky also married the anarchist  russian  aristocrat melania karlovna  a christian of polish german and  ukrainian descent  the marriage lasted until his death in  1943.  czanikovsky served as a military doctor  in the russian army  in world war one and immigrated with his  wife and daughter to palestine  in 1931. he spent the last  years of his life having contracted  leukemia  in the greek patriarchs residence in san  simon  in jerusalem along with his wife who had  become  a devout orthodox christian  czernikovsky is remembered as one of the  most influential  poets of his generation and his works  betray  the influence of his jewish heritage his  love  for biblical figures and the impact of  world literature  sakisaki which was included in  czechnichovsky's  first poetry collection published in  1898  combines a youthful belief in humanism  socialism the fraternity of nations  friendship and zionism it was very  popular  among the zionist socialist youth of  irene's generation  and it is no wonder that she and her  friends  sang it in that transport to hell  my own mother who was just a year older  than irene  also loved that poem and i still recall  her singing it  in my childhood but the song has  remained very popular  not least in israel and it is still sung  in youth movements public events and  social gatherings  and has been performed by an array of  popular singers  there are several other interesting  twists to the story  of sarisaki whose original title  anima amin refers to a pronouncement  in the jewish prayer book siddu based  on the writings of maimonides which  expresses the 13  main principles of jewish faith  the words of anima amin were made into a  popular hasidic tune  the well-known cantor as real fasttag  is said to have sung this hasidic tune  on the train to treblinka where he was  murdered  but unlike this hasidic tune the melody  of sarkisaki sounds distinctly russian  officially the composer of this song is  said to have been  tuvya shlonsky a zionist  from a hasidic family in ukraine and the  father  of the influential hebrew poet editor  and translator  avaham shlonsky one of my own father's  early mentors when he began his own  literary career  it is however quite likely that the tune  of sarkisaki  originated in a russian folk song from  the dawn region  one indication of that is that the very  same melody  was used for another song written by  rabbi avraham  eliao kaplan a major orthodox figure  from lithuania  which he wrote as a 17 year old lad  the song which expresses the spiritual  torment  of a young soul and its longing for god  is officially titled prayer tifila  but it is known and sung to this day  under the name  the sun has set ahama  says it is unlikely that rabbi kaplan  also wrote the tune  we can assume that it was put to music  using the same popular russian tune that  was used  for sakisaki a russian song that served  to animate both a hasidic longing for  god  and a zionist socialist longing for  equality  fraternity and a new jewish generation  in earth israel one can find the  beautiful performance  of the sun has set by the hasidic singer  abhishebrot on youtube  the popularity of sakisaki in israel  to this day found another remarkable  expression  in 2004 during a discussion  in the israeli knesset on the collective  rights of  of the arab population in israel  at one point in the lengthy debate the  arab member  of knesset muhammad baraka proposed  that chernihovsky sakisaki would be a  more appropriate national anthem  than the current one which speaks  primarily  about the longing of jews to zion the  proposal was rejected  but it is a tantalizing idea  perhaps one day charnikhovsky's verse  for my soul still longs for freedom  i have not sold it to the golden calf  for i have still faith in humanity  and in its spirit fierce and bold  sung by jewish youth on the way to  treblinka  will still become the national anthem of  both  jews and palestinians in the state  of israel  [Music]  you

